{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2035 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c602b935795bb794b8d35a6367c2dc788e7fd6e9bb7a81f3eaf4c7cf9419191b" :edb/db-before-t 2034 :edb/db-after-t 2035 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535347 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:32:14.213Z" 13194139535347 true] [17592186133741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345164 13194139535347 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345178 13194139535347 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345185 13194139535347 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345171 13194139535347 true] [17592186134045 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535347 true] [17592186134045 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y = x^{2} + z^{2}$ and the plane $y = 2x$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x, z) ∈ D, f_{1}(x, z) \\le y \\le f_{2}(x, z)}$\nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $D = {(x, z): (x - 1)^{2} + z^{2} \\le 1}$\n2. $D = {(x, z): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 2}$\n3. $f_{1}(x, z) = 2x$ and $f_{2}(x, z) = x^{2} + z^{2}$" 13194139535347 false] [17592186134045 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2x$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$" 13194139535347 true] [17592186134045 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345165 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1089 "q-161137" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$  Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1$  and  ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1113 #uuid "de13cb46-1f12-5e28-a29b-0ed2e0cb7b5f" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345164 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345166 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345167 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345168 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345169 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345170 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1041 "selection" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345166 13194139535347 true] [17592186345165 1110 #uuid "c63e9c4c-b746-58fb-b46a-757809dc57f8" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345166 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345166 1111 #uuid "a2d761b0-c671-52e1-90eb-72def60962b0" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345167 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345167 1111 #uuid "cd6bd396-7d04-576c-a5b0-01d033e756e4" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345168 1034 "I only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345168 1111 #uuid "92b59d57-63e2-55c3-9b82-1451c4cf690f" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345169 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345169 1111 #uuid "b49a53b8-e934-5341-b6fc-3b98dd97e38b" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345170 1034 "II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345170 1111 #uuid "e56db7c4-848e-51db-97ae-653d6d9ef174" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345172 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1089 "q-161250" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/d447e5a00aa324d83a3f513db11ff677fd2db2c7b1b33d8bc2bcfa73b4a08f3f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $y=0,$ and $y=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=z-\\frac{x}{3}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1113 #uuid "fcb442c0-844b-5949-a4f5-a7452e83f2f7" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345171 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345173 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345174 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345175 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345176 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345177 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1041 "selection" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345173 13194139535347 true] [17592186345172 1110 #uuid "a7af75da-772c-58d9-abf5-ca12f131c5ed" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345173 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345173 1111 #uuid "07d86481-33c4-5121-a3c6-fcf128c42579" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345174 1034 "I only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345174 1111 #uuid "6141c947-c959-557a-9f97-724b2ef7bd1b" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345175 1034 "None" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345175 1111 #uuid "f38b6c25-fa19-5043-82b4-1552553103e6" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345176 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345176 1111 #uuid "02637f93-19f8-56be-8818-f2544768daa0" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345177 1034 "II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345177 1111 #uuid "fb0cc83e-c823-56eb-8fb6-b78cfc0d5a85" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345179 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1089 "q-161361" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/f7799ca5801bf019fc5b884590575346a8f298f8e9c91056c13a60c4dd23742d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $x=0,$ and $x=4-y+2z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=4+y-2z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4$" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1113 #uuid "04cc1618-fadc-56de-98a8-91a45ea23917" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345178 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true and statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/3bf8e141067fb6f675933f52e465114d8909d951f6ac85fb09c8dda7efbc2f38.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345180 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345181 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345182 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345183 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345184 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1041 "selection" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345183 13194139535347 true] [17592186345179 1110 #uuid "1a3fb0b9-9ae3-58aa-970b-13fa2f01227b" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345180 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345180 1111 #uuid "b301e79f-fda8-5b13-8fd8-21e0134a7b1d" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345181 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345181 1111 #uuid "8bf7c380-4664-5ce7-8719-651e61e8e04c" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345182 1034 "III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345182 1111 #uuid "95bcc72d-d68b-5bba-94fd-30f0c56ef15a" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345183 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345183 1111 #uuid "20f1a470-90ca-5670-8c8d-01eb69e094ac" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345184 1034 "II only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345184 1111 #uuid "45aebf3b-3923-5fcf-87c6-524be7c98100" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345186 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1089 "q-161386" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between  the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$\n- $a=-3$ and $b=3$" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1113 #uuid "79c3bc30-2865-5506-8ae7-700b9cd28517" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345185 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345187 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345188 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345189 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345190 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345191 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1041 "selection" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345188 13194139535347 true] [17592186345186 1110 #uuid "68a7705b-9ae8-5bc6-a5a1-28fc5e3191a2" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345187 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345187 1111 #uuid "f748ba95-214b-5972-aab3-770ae723e6e8" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345188 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535347 true] [17592186345188 1111 #uuid "5d96e5f6-f043-5909-9f49-38202cb25c9f" 13194139535347 true] [17592186345189 1034 "I only" 13194139535